{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '184,215p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '107,137p;216,245p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md && sed -n '538,680p' packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts\"","aggregated_output":"Expected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n\nThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n\n### 3.6 Type matrix\n\n| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\n| Keyboard order | By x, then series | By x, then series | By category, then series | By slice order | By x, then y |\n| Zoom | Yes | Yes | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\n| Limit that raises a diagnostic | Eight series | Eight series | Eight series | Eight slices | Eight series |\n| Reference lines and bands | Yes; included in the domain; listed in the summary and table caption | Yes | Yes | No | Yes |\n\n### 3.7 Compact variant\n\n`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n\nA compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n\n### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n\n### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n\n```ts\nchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\nchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\nchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\nconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\nchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\nchart.destroy();\n```\n\nFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n\n| Rule | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n\n### 1.6 Diagnostics\n\nThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n\n```\n[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n### 2.4 Document structure\n\n```html\n<figure class=\"axylo\" id=\"ax7\" role=\"group\" aria-labelledby=\"ax7-t\" aria-describedby=\"ax7-s\">\n  <figcaption>\n    <h3 id=\"ax7-t\">Daily sessions by channel</h3>        <!-- level from options; or omitted when labelledBy is used -->\n    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n  </figcaption>\n  <div class=\"axylo-plot\" tabindex=\"0\"> … marks … <div class=\"axylo-text\" aria-hidden=\"true\">tick labels</div></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-toolbar\" role=\"toolbar\" aria-label=\"Chart controls\">   <!-- one tab stop, roving focus -->\n    legend toggles · zoom in · zoom out · reset · pause · view as table\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"axylo-note\" role=\"status\">Values not shown: 2. See table.</p>\n  <div class=\"axylo-live\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-tooltip\" hidden></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-table\" hidden><!-- built when opened: caption, headers, pager --></div>\n</figure>\n```\n\nIds are generated per chart. The plot's role, and how a focused point is conveyed, are not fixed here: M1 runs a spike with three candidate patterns and M4 confirms the choice (section 4).\n\nDefault styles are applied without injecting a `<style>` element, and a standalone stylesheet ships for pages that prefer to load it themselves. Which mechanism passes a strict Content Security Policy is settled by the M7 test with real headers, not assumed.\n\n### 2.5 Repository\n\n```\npackages/axylo/        core library (src/, test/)\npackages/react/        @axylo/react\napps/docs/             documentation site; runnable examples are test fixtures\nbench/                 benchmark harness (04-plan-performance.md), built in M0\n  readonly moreContrast: boolean;\n  readonly paints: PaintBindings;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── rendering ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** Transient decoration drawn above the scene: cursor rule and highlighted points (clipped to the plot), focus ring (not clipped). */\nexport interface Overlay {\n  readonly clip: Rect;\n  readonly clipped: readonly Mark[];\n  readonly unclipped: readonly Mark[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Draws a scene's marks. Two implementations exist for the M1 comparison (canvas, svg); the rest of the\n * library does not know which one is in use.\n *\n * A canvas renderer paints with `theme.paints` (concrete colors) and must be told to draw again when the\n * theme changes. An SVG renderer paints with `bindings` (CSS expressions), so the browser restyles it on a\n * theme change without a call; it switches dash patterns with CSS media queries.\n */\nexport interface MarkRenderer {\n  readonly kind: 'canvas' | 'svg';\n  /** The element to insert into the plot container. It must be aria-hidden. */\n  readonly element: HTMLElement | SVGSVGElement;\n  /** Draws the scene, replacing the previous one. Called at most once per animation frame. */\n  draw(scene: Scene, bindings: PaintBindings, theme: ResolvedTheme, devicePixelRatio: number): void;\n  /** Draws or clears (null) the overlay without redrawing the scene. */\n  drawOverlay(overlay: Overlay | null, bindings: PaintBindings, theme: ResolvedTheme, devicePixelRatio: number): void;\n  destroy(): void;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── lookup ─────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * A datum by identity, not by position, so it still means the same thing after a streaming window slides.\n * Valid within one frame epoch.\n */\nexport type DatumRef =\n  | { readonly kind: 'aligned'; readonly seriesKey: string; /** x position (category: its index at the time; categories do not slide). */ readonly x: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'point'; readonly seriesKey: string; /** source row index */ readonly row: number; /** x position, so a ref to an evicted point can resolve to its nearest surviving neighbour */ readonly x: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'slice'; readonly sliceKey: string };\n\n/** What the pointer is over, by the picking rule of the chart type (spec 3.6). */\nexport type PickTarget =\n  | { readonly kind: 'x'; readonly x: number }                      // line, area, bar: every series at this x\n  | { readonly kind: 'point'; readonly refs: readonly DatumRef[]; readonly count: number }  // scatter: nearest point and those coinciding with it (at most 8; `count` is the full number)\n  | { readonly kind: 'slice'; readonly sliceKey: string };\n\n/** One row of a tooltip or one announced point, as text. All text comes from the frame through the formatters. */\nexport interface DatumText {\n  readonly ref: DatumRef;\n  readonly seriesIndex: number;\n  readonly seriesLabel: string;\n  /** x as the reader sees it. */\n  readonly x: string;\n  /** The value with its unit, or \"No data\" / \"No observation\". */\n  readonly value: string;\n  readonly state: SlotState;\n  /** Share of the whole for a donut slice, formatted; null elsewhere. */\n  readonly percent: string | null;\n  /** Source row index, or -1. */\n  readonly row: number;\n}\n\nexport interface TooltipModel {\n  /** Heading: the x value (with its time zone for instants), the category, or the slice label. */\n  readonly title: string;\n  /** At most eight rows (spec I1). */\n  readonly rows: readonly DatumText[];\n  /** Rows before truncation. */\n  readonly count: number;\n  /** Stack total with \"incomplete\" where it applies, already formatted; null when the chart does not stack. */\n  readonly total: string | null;\n}\n\nexport type StepDirection = 'next' | 'previous' | 'first' | 'last' | 'series-next' | 'series-previous' | 'page-next' | 'page-previous';\n\n/**\n * Exact answers from the frame for one view and projection, independent of how the scene was simplified.\n * Built once per (view, layout); cheap to build: binary search over x for aligned frames, a grid index for scatter.\n */\nexport interface Lookup {\n  /** What is under a pixel of the plot container, or null. */\n  pick(px: number, py: number): PickTarget | null;\n  tooltip(target: PickTarget): TooltipModel;\n  /** The datum to focus when the keyboard takes over from a pointer target. */\n  primary(target: PickTarget): DatumRef | null;\n  datum(ref: DatumRef): DatumText | null;\n  /** Where the datum is drawn, for the cursor and focus ring; null if it is not visible. */\n  anchor(ref: DatumRef): Rect | null;\n  /**\n   * Position for \"point 4 of 120\" (spec A3). Aligned charts: `count` is the number of visible x slots and\n   * `ordinal` the zero-based place of the datum's x among them, whatever its series and whether or not it\n   * holds a value (a \"No data\" slot has a place too). Scatter: place among all visible points in x then y\n   * order. Donut: slice order. -1 only when the datum is not in the current view.\n   */\n  readonly count: number;\n  ordinal(ref: DatumRef): number;\n  /** Keyboard order per chart type (spec 3.6). `from` null starts at the first datum. Skips hidden series. */\n  step(from: DatumRef | null, direction: StepDirection): DatumRef | null;\n  /**\n   * Where focus should be after the data or the view changed. A datum is \"there\" only if its series is\n   * visible and drawable and its x (or point) is inside the current view. If it is: itself. If its series is\n   * hidden but its x is in view: the primary datum at that x. Otherwise the nearest datum of its series in\n   * view after it, else before it; for a donut slice folded into \"Other\", the \"Other\" slice. Else null.\n   * `step` always starts from the resolved datum, so focus can never get stuck on something out of reach.\n   */\n  resolve(ref: DatumRef): DatumRef | null;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── reader model ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** The table of spec A4, built lazily from the frame: nothing is materialized until a page is asked for. */\nexport interface TableModel {\n  readonly caption: string;\n  /** First entry is the x column. */\n  readonly headers: readonly string[];\n  readonly rowCount: number;\n  readonly pageSize: number;\n  /** Cells of one row as text; first cell is the row header. null outside the range. */\n  row(ordinal: number): readonly string[] | null;\n  /** Zero-based page that contains the first row at or after an x value; null if there is none. */\n  pageForX(x: XValue): number | null;\n}\n\n/** Everything a reader gets as text (spec A1, C1). */\nexport interface ReaderModel {\n  /** Accessible name: the title, or a generated one. Empty when the chart is named by `labelledBy`. */\n  readonly name: string;\n  /** Generated summary plus the author's description, including time zone and omission statements. */\n  readonly summary: string;\n  /** The reader's note of spec F12 and L1, or null. */\n  readonly note: string | null;\n  /** null for the compact variant and when the table is turned off. */\n  readonly table: TableModel | null;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── environment ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** Everything outside the options and data that output depends on. Part of every cache key that needs it. */\nexport interface Environment {\n  readonly locale: string;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts | sed -n '1,365p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t/**\n     2\t * Everything a reader uses to explore a full chart (decision 0001; spec I1–I6, A2–A4, A7): one piece of\n     3\t * state, \"what the reader is on\", driven by pointer, touch and keyboard alike, and what is derived from it:\n     4\t * the tooltip, the overlay (drawn with `renderer.drawOverlay`, never by redrawing the scene), the keyboard\n     5\t * proxy's range and value text and the `hover` event. Plus the toolbar (legend, zoom, pause, table), the\n     6\t * table view and the zoom gestures.\n     7\t *\n     8\t * The controller calls `commit` after every frame it draws, with the state that frame shows; this module\n     9\t * never reads a newer state than the marks show. It changes the chart only through `actions`, which go\n    10\t * through the public paths (zoom, the view's hidden set, pause), so every change follows the commit contract.\n    11\t *\n    12\t * The proxy's range always matches the frame on screen: its bounds, value and text are compared with what\n    13\t * the lookup says now and rewritten where they differ (review 2, finding 4). A datum keeps its identity across\n    14\t * commits: on a sliding category axis a reference is moved to the same category's new place, and a datum\n    15\t * that left the window resolves by the lookup's rule (finding 2). No live region is touched by a commit.\n    16\t *\n    17\t * Every listener on the chart's own elements is registered with one abort signal per build, so clearing the\n    18\t * layer or destroying the chart removes them all.\n    19\t */\n    20\timport type {\n    21\t  DatumRef, DatumText, Formatters, Layout, Lookup, MarkRenderer, Overlay, PaintBindings, PickTarget, ReaderModel,\n    22\t  Rect, ResolvedTheme, StepDirection, View, XValue,\n    23\t} from '../core/contracts';\n    24\timport type { Messages } from '../public';\n    25\timport { fill } from '../messages';\n    26\timport {\n    27\t  buildOverlay, canZoomIn, minimumWidth, panWindow, positionAt, refsAt, sameRef, scaleWindow, stepAcross, stepInSeries,\n    28\t  targetBox, targetOf, valueText, PAN_SHARE, ZOOM_FACTOR, type Mode,\n    29\t} from '../core/focus';\n    30\timport type { Figure } from './figure';\n    31\timport { sameTarget } from './pointer';\n    32\timport { createTableView, type TableView } from './table';\n    33\timport { createToolbar, type Toolbar, type ToolbarItem } from './toolbar';\n    34\timport { createTooltip, type TooltipContent } from './tooltip';\n    35\timport { createSwatch, type SwatchSpec } from './swatch';\n    36\timport { sliceText } from '../lookup/text';\n    37\t\n    38\ttype Span = readonly [number, number];\n    39\t\n    40\t/**\n    41\t * What the controls under the plot need. Known before layout, so they are in place when the plot is\n    42\t * measured and their arrival never makes the plot relayout.\n    43\t */\n    44\texport interface ControlsState {\n    45\t  readonly kind: 'line' | 'area' | 'bar' | 'scatter' | 'donut';\n    46\t  readonly view: View;\n    47\t  readonly formatters: Formatters;\n    48\t  readonly messages: Messages;\n    49\t  readonly reader: ReaderModel;\n    50\t  /** The last layout was too small for a plot (spec L8): only the table control is offered. */\n    51\t  readonly tooSmall: boolean;\n    52\t  /** The chart shows data (not loading, empty or error). */\n    53\t  readonly ready: boolean;\n    54\t  /**\n    55\t   * The data can be inspected although nothing is drawn (an empty window, an all-zero donut): the table and\n    56\t   * the donut's slice list stay (spec F8, A4, L7).\n    57\t   */\n    58\t  readonly inspectable?: boolean;\n    59\t  /** A full chart; false for the compact variant, which is a static image (spec C1). */\n    60\t  readonly interactive: boolean;\n    61\t  read